  "body": "Adds `submissions/rust-mio-foovoo/`: a Rust server using raw threads, mio/epoll and a hand-written pipelined Postgres client. `Front: direct`.\n\nOn the eight-core scoring box, defaults select two event loops and eight Postgres connections. Reads share a pool routed by estimated queued work; writes use shared-Sync group commit, and every response waits for its commit. Interleaved bcrypt runs at cost 10 on background workers. JWT HS256 signatures and expiry are checked per request. The README documents dependencies, optimizations, configuration and limitations.\n\nOnly the submission folder is changed, including its pinned lockfile, executable install/build/start scripts and build-output `.gitignore`.\n\n### Existing local validation\n\nNo new tests were run for PR preparation. The submitted implementation matches the source used for the final FEED-w1 checks; subsequent changes are documentation only.\n\n- Local macOS arm64: debug and release `cargo test --locked` each passed 22 tests (one SQL-dump helper ignored and separately run successfully).\n- Ubuntu 24.04 arm64 VM on the M1 Pro: official `test/run.sh submissions/rust-mio-foovoo`, 197/197 passed on each seed (`conduit_dev` and full `conduit_seed`), with defaults after the final FEED-only rewrite.\n- Concurrency/fault-injection checks and both-seed conformance with count routing and batching disabled passed before that rewrite. The FEED change has separate equality (1,988 cases), CPU and generic-plan evidence.\n\n### Local M1 Pro performance\n\nMacBook Pro M1 Pro (10 cores, 32 GB), Lima Ubuntu 24.04 arm64 VM (10 vCPUs, 16 GB), PostgreSQL 18, full seed (500k articles, 100k users). The local harness restricted server plus Postgres to vCPUs 0-3 and four load-generator processes to vCPUs 4-9, using separate loopback addresses. That CPU split was local experiment setup; the submission performs no pinning.\n\nEach probe used 60 s ramp + 60 s measured + 15 s tail:\n\n| Revision / run | Users | req/s | p50 / p95 / p99 (ms) | Errors | Result |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| v4 before FEED w1 (r14, top of ladder) | 80,000 | 19,645.6 | 311.0 / 453.9 / 505.9 | 0% | PASS |\n| Final FEED w1 (r16-w1, short regression probe) | 76,000 | 20,140.5 | 1.8 / 9.4 / 22.6 | 0% | PASS |\n\nBoth reported `lg_limited: no` and `behind=false`. The 76k probe used the submitted implementation; the 80k result predates the FEED-only rewrite. Different host conditions mean the latency difference cannot be attributed to w1. Neither run included a five-minute hold or established a failing upper bound; these are local measurements, not an official challenge score or an eight-core x86_64 benchmark.\n",
